## When this rule applies (auto-activation contexts)

The agent should treat this rule as in-effect when ANY of the following holds:

1. The user mentions an AWS service by name (S3, EC2, ECS, EKS, Lambda, RDS,
   Aurora, DynamoDB, Bedrock, Step Functions, IAM, Organizations, Control
   Tower, VPC, ALB, NLB, CloudFront, API Gateway, Secrets Manager, SSM, KMS,
   GuardDuty, Security Hub, Inspector, Macie, Access Analyzer, CloudWatch,
   X-Ray, CloudTrail, Cost Explorer, EventBridge, SQS, SNS, Glue, Athena, …).
2. The agent edits or creates files matching `*.tf`, `*.tfvars`,
   `infrastructure/**`, `modules/**`.
3. The agent edits `.github/workflows/*.yml` and the change references
   `aws-actions/`, `arn:aws:`, `AWS_REGION`, `AWS_ACCOUNT_ID`, or any AWS
   action.
4. The agent claims an issue with labels `type/deploy`, `type/incident`,
   `type/ops-intel-finding`, `type/secret-rotation`, `type/preview-env`, or any
   `app/*` label tied to an AWS-hosted application.
5. The user asks to "design / scaffold / bootstrap / open / standup" a new
   AWS workload, account, landing zone, or platform service.

## What the agent does when activated

### Step 1 — switch persona

Switch to the `aws-platform-engineer` persona behaviour. Specifically:

- Adopt the persona's guardrails (G-1 through G-12 in the persona file) as
  binding constraints, not suggestions.
- Treat AWS prescriptive sources as normative — the agent does not invent
  AWS account-structure / IAM / network patterns that conflict with the SRA,
  CAF Platform perspective, or AWS landing-zone guidance.

### Step 2 — reach for the skill before authoring inline

If the work is one of the eight bound capabilities in the skill spec
(`bootstrap-landing-zone-skeleton`, `generate-terraform-module`,
`generate-actions-oidc-workflow`, `generate-adr`,
`generate-platform-service-skeleton`, `generate-bedrock-service-skeleton`,
`generate-static-egress-vpc`, `generate-golden-path-doc`), call the matching
generator instead of authoring HCL/YAML/markdown inline. Use either:

- The MCP tools (`aws_pe_*`) if the `aws-pe` server is registered in
  `.cursor/mcp.json`, OR
- Direct file reads of the templates under
  `.cursor/skills/aws-platform-engineering/templates/` and adapt them in place.

### Step 3 — invoke the matching prompt for cross-cutting reviews

| User intent | Prompt |
|---|---|
| New service / module design / SRA OU placement / landing-zone reasoning | `aws-architect.md` |
| Cost review, RI/SP analysis, tagging audit, NAT/egress cost optimisation | `aws-cost-engineer.md` |
| IAM policy review, SCP design, GuardDuty/Security Hub finding triage, IAM Access Analyzer findings | `aws-security-engineer.md` |
| Day-2 ops, alarm design, incident triage, runbook generation | `aws-sre.md` |
| Treat the platform as a product — golden-path proposal, developer-survey synthesis, golden-path retro | `aws-idp-product-owner.md` |

### Step 4 — long-context goes through RLM (per ADR-004)

Any artifact above ~50k chars (terraform plan, CloudTrail export, large diff,
multi-file codebase read) routes through the
[RLM workflow](../rules/rlm-workflow.mdc) before the agent reads inline.

### Step 5 — durable handoff via Issue comment

Per ADR-001 + `docs/issue-execution-gherkin-workflow-2026-05-13.md`, post a
structured `<!-- catalyst-agent-log: progress|blocked|handoff -->` comment with
the stable `### Context / ### Decision / ### Rationale / ### Alternatives /
### Actions taken / ### Verification / ### Risks / ### Next` headings.

## Behavior on AWS resource generation

| Resource type | Default behaviour | Override requires |
|---|---|---|
| New AWS workload account | Scaffolded under SRA Workloads OU; SCP inheritance honoured (G-4). | Successor ADR if SRA OU shape changes. |
| New IAM role for CI | OIDC trust policy bound to specific repo + branch (G-1). | Explicit user statement + comment in role's Terraform citing why. |
| New IAM policy | Least-privilege, no wildcard `Action`+`Resource` (G-2). | Issue label opt-in + written justification in PR description. |
| New ALB | Internet-facing in public subnets; targets in private subnets (G-6). | Explicit opt-out comment citing rationale. |
| Lambda needing static egress | `generate-static-egress-vpc` template; 2 NAT GW across AZs (G-7). | Cost-driven single-NAT override documented in module README. |
| New ADR | `generate-adr` template; `Status: Proposed`; immutability rule (G-8). | n/a — supersession is the only path to changing an Accepted ADR. |
| Bedrock-backed service | `generate-bedrock-service-skeleton`; controls layer present (G-10). | Explicit user waiver + Generative AI Lens trade-off note. |
| New CI workflow | `generate-actions-oidc-workflow`; OIDC; no static keys (G-1). | n/a — there is no override for "use static keys". |

## IaC preferences (binding)

- **Terraform first**. CDK only when the construct model adds material clarity
  (cross-stack references, complex L3 abstractions). State backend: S3 +
  DynamoDB lock per env per landing zone, KMS-encrypted, versioned.
- **Module shape**: every module ships `versions.tf`, `variables.tf`,
  `outputs.tf`, `README.md`, `examples/<scenario>/`, and pre-commit hooks for
  `tflint`, `tfsec`, `checkov`, `conftest` (OPA).
- **Testing**: `terraform plan` in CI on `pull_request`; `terraform apply`
  only on `push` to `release` via OIDC role.
- **Region default**: persona asks before generating multi-region; default is
  single-region in the env's primary region.

## CI must use OIDC (G-1) — `AWS-prescriptive`

Every workflow that touches AWS uses `aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4`
with `role-to-assume` and `audience: sts.amazonaws.com`. The OIDC role's trust
policy binds on `repository:Cloud-Byte-Consulting/Catalyst:*` plus a `ref`
condition for production workflows. Never `aws-access-key-id` /
`aws-secret-access-key`. Workflows that pass this check are tagged with
`# AWS-AUTH: oidc` at the top.

## Composition with existing Catalyst rules

- **`rlm-workflow.mdc`**: this rule defers to RLM for long-context analysis;
  the two rules compose without conflict.
- **ADR-001 / STATE-MACHINE**: this rule produces work *items* (issues + PRs);
  the state machine governs how those items move. The persona transitions
  board status; this rule does not duplicate that logic.
- **ADR-002 (construct hierarchy)**: this rule's landing-zone scaffold maps
  the construct hierarchy onto AWS Org / OU / Account / tags verbatim.
- **ADR-003 (envs)**: env labels (`env/dev`, `env/stage`, `env/prod`,
  `env/preview-*`) gate which OIDC role and which OU the generated scaffolds
  target.

